/**
\defgroup distrib_properizing_tail Properizing tails
\brief Properizing tails that replace the flat region beyond the last knot of the MCP and SCAD penalties so that their negative exponentials integrate to one.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/properizing_tail.hpp>` in namespace `laplace::distrib`, this module provides the three properizing tails used as the template parameter of \ref laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib "mcp_distrib" (\ref distrib_mcp "Properized MCP") and \ref laplace::distrib::scad_distrib "scad_distrib" (\ref distrib_scad "Properized SCAD"): \ref laplace::distrib::student_tail "student_tail" (their default), \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian_tail "gaussian_tail", and \ref laplace::distrib::logistic_tail "logistic_tail". Every member that evaluates a tail or its proximal roots is marked `LAPLACE_HD`, so it runs on the host and inside Kokkos kernels; `start_value()` runs on the host only. Against SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) and mpmath (mpmath) references, the mixed partials of the tail exponent agree within \f$8.1 \times 10^{-13}\f$, the tail constants and partial integrals agree with 40-digit quadrature within \f$1.3 \times 10^{-27}\f$, the curvature moments agree with 60-digit quadrature within \f$1.8 \times 10^{-43}\f$, and across the MCP and SCAD distributions the worst measured error of the log-density and its derivatives to order \f$6\f$ is \f$163\f$ ulp of the scale of their order; the measures are those of \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured", and the figures are in the section "Accuracy and limits" below.

## Definition and comparison of the three tails

Zhang's MCP (Zhang, 2010) and Fan and Li's SCAD (Fan and Li, 2001) penalties are flat beyond their last knot. A density proportional to \f$e^{-\rho}\f$ therefore has an infinite integral for every parameter value, so the penalty cannot serve as a log-density as it stands. A properizing tail replaces the flat part beyond the last knot by an exponent that grows, leaving the penalty untouched on the inner pieces.

Let \f$u = |y - \mu|\f$ be the distance of an observation \f$y \in \mathbb{R}\f$ from the location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$, and let \f$u_0\f$ be the law's last knot, equal to \f$\gamma\lambda\f$ for the MCP and \f$a\lambda\f$ for the SCAD. With \f$x = u - u_0 \ge 0\f$ the distance beyond the knot and \f$\theta\f$ the tail's own parameters, a tail defines the exponent
\f[
H(x; \theta) = \kappa(\theta)\,\Psi\!\left(\frac{x}{\varsigma(\theta)}\right), \qquad x \ge 0,
\f]
where \f$\Psi\f$ is the shape function, \f$\kappa(\theta)\f$ is an overall factor, and \f$\varsigma(\theta)\f$ is a monomial in the tail's parameters. Every tail satisfies \f$H(0) = H'(0) = 0\f$, which keeps \f$q\f$ and \f$q'\f$ continuous at the knot.

| Tail | \f$\Psi(z)\f$ | \f$\varsigma(\theta)\f$ | \f$\kappa(\theta)\f$ | Parameters |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `gaussian_tail` | \f$z^2/2\f$ | \f$V^{1/2}\f$ | \f$1\f$ | `tail_sigma2` \f$= V > 0\f$ |
| `student_tail` | \f$\log(1 + z^2)\f$ | \f$s\nu^{1/2}\f$ | \f$(\nu + 1)/2\f$ | `tail_scale` \f$= s > 0\f$, `tail_df` \f$= \nu > 0\f$ |
| `logistic_tail` | \f$2\log\cosh z\f$ | \f$s\f$ | \f$1\f$ | `tail_scale` \f$= s > 0\f$ |

Beyond the weight of the tail, the three forms differ in the score \f$q'\f$ far from the location. The penalties exist because \f$q' = 0\f$ beyond the knot leaves large deviations unpenalized. With `gaussian_tail`, \f$q'(x) = x/V\f$ grows without bound, so beyond \f$x = \lambda V\f$ the law shrinks more than a Laplace distribution of rate \f$\lambda\f$ (\ref distrib_laplace2 "laplace2"). With `logistic_tail`, the score tends to \f$2/s\f$, so when \f$2/s \le \lambda\f$ that tail never shrinks more than the rate-\f$\lambda\f$ Laplace distribution. With `student_tail`, the score is \f$(\nu + 1)x / (\nu s^2 + x^2)\f$ and tends to zero, so the unpenalized tail behaviour survives up to \f$O(1/x)\f$; this is why `student_tail` is the default. Measured at \f$s = 1\f$ and \f$\nu = 3\f$ (with \f$V = 1\f$ for `gaussian_tail`), where \f$x\f$ is the distance beyond the knot, the scores are as follows.

| \f$x\f$ | Gaussian \f$q'\f$ | Logistic \f$q'\f$ | Student \f$q'\f$ | Student \f$x q'\f$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| \f$10\f$ | \f$10\f$ | \f$2\f$ | \f$0.3883495\f$ | \f$3.8834951\f$ |
| \f$100\f$ | \f$100\f$ | \f$2\f$ | \f$0.03998800\f$ | \f$3.9988004\f$ |
| \f$1000\f$ | \f$1000\f$ | \f$2\f$ | \f$0.003999988\f$ | \f$3.999988\f$ |
| \f$10000\f$ | \f$10000\f$ | \f$2\f$ | \f$0.000399999988\f$ | \f$3.9999999\f$ |

The Gaussian tail is the limit of the Student tail as \f$\nu\f$ grows at a fixed scale \f$s\f$ with \f$V = s^2\f$. At \f$y\f$ equal to \f$5.1\f$ beyond the knot, the difference of the log-densities is \f$9.15 \times 10^{-1}\f$, \f$1.046 \times 10^{-2}\f$, \f$1.047 \times 10^{-4}\f$, and \f$1.049 \times 10^{-6}\f$ at \f$\nu = 10^2, 10^4, 10^6, 10^8\f$, so \f$\nu\f$ times the difference settles at \f$104.9\f$ and the difference falls like \f$1/\nu\f$. In the Cauchy case \f$\nu = 1\f$, the Student tail gives \f$T = s\pi/2\f$.

## Normalizing constants and partial integrals

Both the MCP and SCAD laws have normalizing constant \f$Z = 2[A(\lambda, p) + B(\lambda, p)\,T]\f$, where \f$p\f$ is \f$\gamma\f$ for MCP or \f$a\f$ for SCAD, and \f$T = \int_0^\infty e^{-H(v; \theta)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ is the tail's own constant. The tail's parameters enter \f$Z\f$ through \f$T\f$ alone. For the cumulative functions, each tail also supplies its partial integral \f$G(x) = \int_x^\infty e^{-H(v; \theta)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ and its logarithm.

| Tail | \f$T\f$ | \f$\int_x^\infty e^{-H(v; \theta)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gaussian_tail` | \f$\sqrt{\pi V/2}\f$ | \f$2\sqrt{\pi V/2}\,\Phi(-x/\sqrt{V})\f$ |
| `student_tail` | \f$\dfrac{s}{2}\sqrt{\pi\nu}\,\dfrac{\Gamma(\nu/2)}{\Gamma((\nu+1)/2)}\f$ | \f$T\,I_w(\nu/2, 1/2)\f$, with \f$w = \varsigma^2/(\varsigma^2 + x^2)\f$ |
| `logistic_tail` | \f$s\f$ | \f$s(1 - \tanh(x/s))\f$ |

Here \f$\Phi\f$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function (\ref normal "normal"), and \f$I_w\f$ is the regularized incomplete beta function (\ref incomplete_beta "incomplete beta"). For `student_tail`, the substitution \f$v = \varsigma\tau\f$ followed by \f$w = 1/(1 + \tau^2)\f$ turns the tail integral into a beta integral.

Two numerical precautions govern the partial integrals. First, for `student_tail`, whichever of the two beta arguments \f$w = \varsigma^2 / (\varsigma^2 + x^2)\f$ and \f$1 - w = x^2 / (\varsigma^2 + x^2)\f$ is smaller is formed directly, never by subtracting the other from one. Just beyond the knot, \f$1 - w\f$ is of the order \f$x^2/\varsigma^2\f$, which the quotient for \f$w\f$ rounds away; when \f$x^2 \le \varsigma^2\f$, the implementation therefore forms \f$x^2 / (\varsigma^2 + x^2)\f$ and calls the complementary incomplete beta function. Second, for `logistic_tail`, \f$1 - \tanh z\f$ is about \f$2e^{-2z}\f$, so forming it by subtraction gives zero from \f$z = x/s\f$ of about \f$19\f$, where it falls below the spacing of \f$1\f$; the partial integral is written as \f$2se^{-2z} / (1 + e^{-2z})\f$ and its logarithm as \f$\log(2s) - 2z - \log(1 + e^{-2z})\f$.

## Mixed partial derivatives

Two identities carry every mixed partial derivative in the tail piece of the penalty, both derived and checked against SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) in `tools/reference/properized_tail_forms.py`.

Through the knot, both laws have \f$u_0 = p\lambda\f$. For any smooth function \f$F\f$ of \f$x = u - p\lambda\f$,
\f[
\partial_u^j \partial_\lambda^i \partial_p^m F(x) = \sum_{r=\max(i,m)}^{i+m} c_r(i,m)\,F^{(j+r)}(x), \qquad
c_r(i,m) = \binom{i}{m + i - r}(-1)^m \frac{m!}{(r - i)!}\lambda^{r-i}(-p)^{r-m}.
\f]
The derivatives in \f$u\f$ only shift the order of differentiation of \f$F\f$.

In the tail's own parameters, write \f$\mathcal{D} = \varsigma\,\partial_\varsigma\f$ and \f$E = z\,\partial_z\f$, where \f$z = x/\varsigma\f$. Every parameter \f$\theta_j\f$ enters \f$\varsigma\f$ only through the monomial \f$\varsigma = \prod_j \theta_j^{e_j}\f$, so \f$\theta_j\partial_{\theta_j} = e_j\mathcal{D}\f$. Signed Stirling numbers of the first kind \f$s_1(n, k)\f$ turn \f$\partial^n\f$ into \f$(\theta\partial_\theta)^k\f$, and Stirling numbers of the second kind \f$S_2(q, j)\f$ express \f$E^q f = \sum_{j=0}^q S_2(q, j)\,z^j f^{(j)}\f$:
\f[
\partial_{\theta_1}^{n_1}\partial_{\theta_2}^{n_2} G
= \theta_1^{-n_1}\theta_2^{-n_2} \sum_{k_1=0}^{n_1}\sum_{k_2=0}^{n_2} s_1(n_1, k_1)\,s_1(n_2, k_2)\,e_1^{k_1} e_2^{k_2}\,\mathcal{D}^{k_1+k_2} G,
\f]
\f[
\mathcal{D}^m\bigl[\varsigma^{-r}\Psi^{(r)}(z)\bigr]
= (-1)^m \varsigma^{-r} \sum_{q=0}^m \binom{m}{q} r^{m-q} \sum_{j=0}^q S_2(q, j)\,z^j\,\Psi^{(r+j)}(z),
\f]
because \f$\mathcal{D}\f$ acts on a function of \f$z\f$ as \f$-E\f$ and on \f$\varsigma^{-r}\f$ as multiplication by \f$-r\f$. Since \f$\kappa\f$ is constant for `gaussian_tail` and `logistic_tail` and affine in \f$\nu\f$ for `student_tail`, the Leibniz rule over \f$\kappa\Psi\f$ has at most two terms. Each component is therefore a finite sum of values of \f$\Psi\f$ with integer coefficients, without Bell polynomials, evaluated through one code path for all three tails with only a few scalars on the stack.

The highest order of \f$\Psi\f$ reached is \f$r + j\f$ with \f$r \le J + K\f$ and \f$j \le K\f$, which is \f$18\f$ at \f$J = K = 6\f$, where \f$J\f$ is the order in \f$y\f$ and \f$K\f$ is the total order in the parameters. For `gaussian_tail`, \f$\Psi'(z) = z\f$, \f$\Psi''(z) = 1\f$, and higher orders vanish. For `student_tail`, \f$\Psi^{(k)}(z) = N_k(z)\,\omega^k\f$ with \f$\omega = 1/(1 + z^2)\f$, \f$N_1(z) = 2z\f$, and \f$N_{k+1}(z) = N_k'(z)(1 + z^2) - 2kzN_k(z)\f$; each term of degree \f$j\f$ with coefficient \f$c_j\f$ is assembled as \f$c_j(z\omega)^j\omega^{k-j}\f$, where \f$|z\omega| \le 1/2\f$ and \f$\omega \le 1\f$ for every \f$z\f$, so nothing overflows at any order. For `logistic_tail`, \f$\Psi^{(k)}(z) = 2\,\partial^{k-1}\tanh z\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$, written in \f$g = \operatorname{sech}^2 z\f$ and \f$t = \tanh z\f$ as \f$R_n(g)\f$ for odd \f$n = k - 1\f$ and \f$t\,R_n(g)\f$ for even \f$n\f$, with \f$R_0 = 1\f$, \f$R_{n+1} = -2g R_n'\f$ from odd to even \f$n\f$, and \f$R_{n+1} = g R_n - 2g(1 - g)R_n'\f$ from even to odd \f$n\f$. Every \f$R_n\f$ with \f$n \ge 1\f$ vanishes at \f$g = 0\f$, so nothing cancels in the far tail, and \f$g\f$ is built from \f$4e^{-2|z|}/(1 + e^{-2|z|})^2\f$ rather than from \f$1 - \tanh^2 z\f$.

The table of \f$\log Z\f$ runs over \f$(\lambda, p, \theta_1)\f$ for a one-parameter tail and over \f$(\lambda, p, s, \nu)\f$ for `student_tail`. The four-variable case uses a four-variable logarithm recursion over a triangularly packed table, which at order \f$6\f$ holds \f$210\f$ entries against the \f$2401\f$ of the square box. For `student_tail`, the mixed partials of \f$T = s\,G(\nu)\f$ are formed through \f$\log G(\nu)\f$, whose derivatives in \f$\nu\f$ are elementary plus one polygamma difference per order, followed by the scalar exponential recursion.

## Curvature moments

To evaluate the expected Fisher information of \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP" and \ref distrib_scad "the Properized SCAD", each tail supplies three integrals of second derivatives of \f$H\f$ against the tail's own weight:
\f[
\int_0^\infty H''(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x, \qquad
\int_0^\infty \partial_x\partial_{\theta_j} H(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x, \qquad
\int_0^\infty \partial_{\theta_i}\partial_{\theta_j} H(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x,
\f]
together with the identity \f$\int_0^\infty H'(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x = 1\f$, which follows from \f$H(0) = 0\f$. Writing \f$H = \kappa\Psi(x/\varsigma)\f$ with \f$\varsigma = \prod_j \theta_j^{e_j}\f$ a monomial in the tail's parameters and \f$z = x/\varsigma\f$, each of the three reduces to the tail constant \f$T\f$, to \f$S_0 = T/\varsigma\f$, and to the three moments
\f[
P_k = \int_0^\infty z^k\,\Psi''(z)\,e^{-\kappa\Psi(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z, \qquad k = 0, 1, 2,
\f]
which `psi_curvature_moment(k)` returns. No integral of \f$\Psi\f$ itself appears, because \f$\kappa\f$ is affine in its parameter and its second derivative vanishes. With \f$\eta_j = e_j/\theta_j\f$ and \f$\kappa_j = \partial\kappa/\partial\theta_j\f$, integrating \f$\int_0^\infty \Psi'(z)\,e^{-\kappa\Psi(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z = 1/\kappa\f$ and \f$\int_0^\infty z\,\Psi'(z)\,e^{-\kappa\Psi(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z = S_0/\kappa\f$ by parts turns the three tail integrals into
\f[
\int_0^\infty H'' e^{-H}\,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{\kappa}{\varsigma} P_0, \qquad
\int_0^\infty \partial_x\partial_{\theta_j} H\,e^{-H}\,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{\kappa_j}{\kappa} - \eta_j(\kappa P_1 + 1),
\f]
\f[
\int_0^\infty \partial_{\theta_i}\partial_{\theta_j} H\,e^{-H}\,\mathrm{d}x = \varsigma\left[-\frac{\kappa_i\eta_j + \kappa_j\eta_i}{\kappa} S_0 + [i = j]\,\frac{\eta_i S_0}{\theta_i} + \eta_i\eta_j(S_0 + \kappa P_2)\right].
\f]

| Tail | \f$P_0\f$ | \f$P_1\f$ | \f$P_2\f$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `gaussian_tail` | \f$\sqrt{\pi/2}\f$ | \f$1\f$ | \f$\sqrt{\pi/2}\f$ |
| `logistic_tail` | \f$4/3\f$ | \f$(4\log 2 - 1)/3\f$ | \f$\pi^2/9 - 2/3\f$ |
| `student_tail` | \f$2\nu S_0/(\nu + 3)\f$ | \f$2(\nu - 1)/[(\nu + 1)(\nu + 3)]\f$ | \f$2(\nu - 3)S_0/[(\nu + 1)(\nu + 3)]\f$ |

The moments of `gaussian_tail` are the Gaussian moments, since \f$\Psi''(z) = 1\f$. Those of `logistic_tail` follow from \f$\Psi''(z)\,e^{-\Psi(z)} = 2\operatorname{sech}^4 z\f$, with the two non-rational constants identified from 50-digit quadrature with mpmath (mpmath) and confirmed to \f$1.3 \times 10^{-51}\f$. Those of `student_tail` are Beta integrals \f$\int_0^\infty z^{2m}(1 + z^2)^{-\alpha}\,\mathrm{d}z = \tfrac{1}{2} B(m + \tfrac{1}{2}, \alpha - m - \tfrac{1}{2})\f$ reduced to \f$S_0\f$ times a rational function of \f$\nu\f$, the odd power at \f$k = 1\f$ making \f$P_1\f$ rational on its own. No new special function is evaluated anywhere in the expected information: the closed form reads only Dawson's integral with its first two derivatives, the tail's own constant, and rational arithmetic.

## Proximal roots

For the proximal operator in the observation (\ref distrib_mcp "Properized MCP", \ref distrib_scad "Properized SCAD", \ref penalty_prox "Proximal operators"), each tail supplies the stationary points of the proximal objective beyond the law's last knot \f$u_0\f$ through `prox_roots(w, t, z)`. Here \f$w = U - u_0\f$ is the signed distance of \f$U = |v - \mu|\f$ beyond the knot, \f$t > 0\f$ is the step, and \f$z = |y - \mu| - u_0\f$ is the excess of the candidate beyond the knot. The stationary equation is
\f[
t\,H'(z) + z - w = 0, \qquad z > 0.
\f]
The member writes all positive roots into the output array `z`, which must hold at least four doubles, in ascending order, and returns their count. Each tail has a root with \f$z > 0\f$ only when \f$w > 0\f$, and returns \f$0\f$ otherwise. The ascending order is what lets the caller's tie rule keep the smallest minimizer: its own candidates below the knot are already in that order.

| Tail | Stationary equation | Roots for \f$w > 0\f$ | Shrinkage \f$w - z\f$ as \f$w\f$ grows |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `gaussian_tail` | \f$(t/V + 1)z - w = 0\f$ | one, \f$wV/(V + t)\f$ | \f$wt/(V + t)\f$, without bound |
| `student_tail` | \f$z^3 - wz^2 + (\varsigma^2 + 2t\kappa)z - w\varsigma^2 = 0\f$ | one or three | \f$2t\kappa/w\f$, tending to zero |
| `logistic_tail` | \f$(2t/s)\tanh(z/s) + z - w = 0\f$ | one | \f$2t/s\f$, tending to a constant |

### Gaussian tail

For `gaussian_tail`, \f$H'(z) = z/V\f$ makes the stationary equation linear. When \f$w > 0\f$, it has the single positive root \f$z = wV / (V + t)\f$, and \f$0\f$ roots otherwise. The shrinkage \f$w - z = wt / (V + t)\f$ grows linearly with \f$w\f$ without bound.

### Student tail

For `student_tail`, \f$H'(z) = 2\kappa z / (\varsigma^2 + z^2)\f$ rises to \f$\kappa/\varsigma\f$ at \f$z = \varsigma\f$ and falls back to zero. The proximal objective can therefore hold two local minima beyond the knot, and the caller compares the objective values at all candidates to pick between them. Clearing the denominator turns \f$t\,H'(z) + z - w = 0\f$ into the cubic
\f[
z^3 - wz^2 + (\varsigma^2 + 2t\kappa)z - w\varsigma^2 = 0.
\f]
When \f$w > 0\f$, its coefficients alternate in sign, so by Descartes' rule of signs it has one or three positive roots, and the polynomial in \f$-z\f$ has no sign change at all, so it has no negative root: every real root is positive and none is discarded. The roots come from the trigonometric form when the discriminant of the depressed cubic is positive and from Cardano's formula otherwise, and each is then refined by Newton's method on \f$t\,H'(z) + z - w\f$, which near a root is better conditioned than the cubic, until the step no longer moves the iterate. The refinement is not cosmetic: the root the trigonometric form supplies can sit \f$4.4 \times 10^{6}\f$ ulp from the converged one.

The discriminant of this cubic is a difference of two terms of size \f$w^6\f$ that agree to leading order, so its computed sign is not to be trusted for a large \f$w\f$: at \f$w = 10^{11}\f$ it comes out positive where the cubic has one real root, which would send the branch test to the trigonometric form and fill the output with three numbers that are not roots. Whenever \f$w > \varsigma\f$, Newton's method therefore also runs from \f$w\f$ in the region \f$z > \varsigma\f$, where the left-hand side increases, and adds the root near \f$w\f$ regardless of the branch test. A test checks the stationarity out to \f$U = 10^{15}\f$. As \f$w\f$ grows, the shrinkage \f$w - z\f$ behaves like \f$2t\kappa/w\f$ and falls to zero.

### Logistic tail

For `logistic_tail`, \f$H'(z) = (2/s)\tanh(z/s)\f$, so \f$t\,H'(z) + z - w\f$ is increasing and concave on \f$z > 0\f$ and has a single root when \f$w > 0\f$. Newton's method starts from \f$\max(0, w - 2t/s)\f$, where \f$t\,H'(z) + z - w \le 0\f$, rises monotonically to the root, and stops once the step no longer increases the iterate, which is at rounding level. As \f$w\f$ grows, the shrinkage \f$w - z\f$ settles at the constant \f$2t/s\f$.

### Tail choice for the proximal operator

A point far beyond the last knot carries no information about the penalty's shape, and the proximal operator of the classical MCP and SCAD penalties leaves it alone because those penalties are flat there. Of the three properizing tails, only `student_tail` agrees with that behaviour in the limit because its pull \f$w - z\f$ falls to zero, whereas `logistic_tail` shifts distant points by the constant \f$2t/s\f$ and `gaussian_tail` shrinks them without bound.

## Tail structure

Each tail is an aggregate holding its own parameters (`tail_sigma2` for `gaussian_tail`, `tail_scale` and `tail_df` for `student_tail`, and `tail_scale` for `logistic_tail`), `n_tail_params`, and `tail_params`. It supplies \f$\Psi^{(k)}\f$ at a runtime order via `psi_deriv()`, the exponents of its parameters in \f$\varsigma\f$ via `exponent()`, its \f$\kappa\f$ via `kappa()` (affine in one parameter at most), the constant \f$T\f$ with its mixed partials and its logarithm (`tail_const()`, `const_mixed()`, `const_table()`, `log_tail_const()`), the curvature moments \f$P_0, P_1, P_2\f$ via `psi_curvature_moment()`, the partial integral \f$\int_x^\infty e^{-H(v; \theta)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ with its logarithm (`upper()`, `log_upper()`), the positive stationary points beyond the knot via `prox_roots()`, a rough spread for quadratures (`scale_hint()`), and a starting value from a sample summary (`start_value()`). Whereas `const_mixed()` returns one mixed partial of \f$T\f$, `const_table()` writes the whole table of mixed partials of \f$T\f$ up to a runtime order at once. Calling `const_table()` once avoids rebuilding the derivatives of \f$G(\nu)\f$ and \ref laplace::math::lgamma_half_ratio "lgamma_half_ratio()" (\ref stirling "Stirling's error") at each multi-index of `student_tail`, taking 3272 ns against 7258 ns per call for the expected information of `mcp_distrib` with `student_tail`.

## Accuracy and limits

Against SymPy's own derivatives at the exact rationals of the doubles used by the transcribed route (`tools/reference/properized_tail_forms.py`), the chain through the knot agrees within \f$1.0 \times 10^{-15}\f$ over every multi-index of total order up to \f$6\f$, the components of \f$H\f$ agree within \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$, \f$2.7 \times 10^{-14}\f$, and \f$3.0 \times 10^{-13}\f$ for the Gaussian, Student, and logistic tails, the two identities composed agree within \f$8.9 \times 10^{-16}\f$, \f$8.1 \times 10^{-13}\f$, and \f$1.1 \times 10^{-14}\f$, and the four-variable logarithm of \f$Z\f$ agrees within \f$6.2 \times 10^{-15}\f$. Against mpmath (mpmath), the two transcriptions of \f$\Psi^{(k)}\f$ agree to order \f$18\f$ within \f$4.6 \times 10^{-14}\f$ and \f$3.5 \times 10^{-13}\f$ relative over \f$z\f$ from \f$0\f$ to \f$200\f$. Against 40-digit quadrature of \f$e^{-H}\f$, the closed \f$T\f$ agrees within \f$1.4 \times 10^{-41}\f$ for `gaussian_tail` and exactly for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail`; its mixed partials to total order \f$6\f$ agree with SymPy within \f$1.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$, \f$1.6 \times 10^{-16}\f$, and exactly. Over \f$x\f$ from \f$0\f$ to \f$20\f$, the partial integrals agree with 40-digit quadrature within \f$3.9 \times 10^{-40}\f$, \f$4.1 \times 10^{-41}\f$, and \f$1.3 \times 10^{-27}\f$ for the three tails. Against 60-digit quadrature of their definitions, the closed curvature moments \f$P_0, P_1, P_2\f$ from `psi_curvature_moment()` agree to \f$1.8 \times 10^{-43}\f$, and `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_info.py` checks them together with the three tail curvature integrals against 200-bit quadrature.

Across both the MCP and SCAD distributions (`tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_tails.cpp` and `tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad.cpp`, host in both gcc and CUDA builds), the closed \f$Z\f$ agrees with 400-bit quadrature \f$Z = 2\int_0^\infty e^{-q(u)}\,\mathrm{d}u\f$ to \f$1.1 \times 10^{-48}\f$ over all \f$36\f$ cases of `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad.py`, which also verifies that each density integrates to one. On the inner pieces, both laws equal `mcp_penalty` and `scad_penalty` bit for bit in value for every tail and within \f$2\varepsilon\f$ of \f$\max(|\mathrm{value}|, 1)\f$ in the derivatives (test bound \f$8\varepsilon\f$, where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is double-precision machine epsilon), while the derivatives in the tail's own parameters are zero there. The mass by panel quadrature with every knot declared as a cut is out by \f$3.3 \times 10^{-16}\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-13}\f$) for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail` and by \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-15}\f$) for `gaussian_tail`; the increment of \f$F\f$ against the quadrature of the density is out by \f$8.7 \times 10^{-17}\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-10}\f$ relative) for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail`.

Against mpmath references computed at 300 and 400 bits (`tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad.py`, over \f$74184\f$ values for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail`, and separately for `gaussian_tail`), the worst measured errors and test bounds are listed below. The log-density errors are in ulp of the scale of their order (the largest component of that order at the point), and the cumulative function errors are in ulp of \f$\max(|\log F|, 1)\f$; the measures are those of \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". The worst point at order \f$6\f$ is the SCAD with `logistic_tail` at \f$\lambda = 3\f$, \f$a = 1.1\f$, and \f$s = 0.5\f$.

| Quantity | Student and logistic, measured | Student and logistic, bound | Gaussian, measured | Gaussian, bound |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Log-density, order 0 | \f$2\f$ ulp | \f$4\f$ ulp | \f$3.66\f$ ulp | \f$6\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 1 | \f$6\f$ ulp | \f$10\f$ ulp | \f$8\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 2 | \f$6\f$ ulp | \f$10\f$ ulp | \f$6.3\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 3 | \f$10\f$ ulp | \f$16\f$ ulp | \f$4.45\f$ ulp | \f$14\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 4 | \f$9\f$ ulp | \f$16\f$ ulp | \f$11.2\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 5 | \f$25\f$ ulp | \f$40\f$ ulp | \f$40.2\f$ ulp | \f$64\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 6 | \f$163\f$ ulp | \f$250\f$ ulp | \f$163\f$ ulp | \f$250\f$ ulp |
| \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ | \f$2\f$ ulp | \f$4\f$ ulp | \f$2.46\f$ ulp | \f$4\f$ ulp |
| Normalizing constant \f$Z\f$ | \f$2\f$ ulp | \f$3\f$ ulp | \f$1.33\f$ ulp | \f$2\f$ ulp |

In the limit, \f$\gamma \to 0\f$ for the MCP and \f$\lambda \to 0\f$ for the SCAD leave no inner region and give \f$e^{-H}/(2T)\f$, while sending the last knot to infinity makes the tail's mass vanish and gives the Laplace distribution of rate \f$\lambda\f$. Both laws evaluate the expected information and its parameter derivatives to order four in closed form (`expected_info()` and `expected_info_dk<K>()`), whereas the quantile has no closed form with any tail and is served by \ref distrib_generic "the generic route". For `student_tail`, \f$\log T = \tfrac{1}{2}\log(\pi\nu) - \log 2 - R(\nu/2)\f$ with \f$R(a) = \log\Gamma(a + \tfrac{1}{2}) - \log\Gamma(a)\f$ the half-step log-gamma ratio of \ref stirling "Stirling's error", and the \f$\nu\f$-derivatives of \f$\log T\f$ read one value of \f$R^{(k)}\f$ each. Written instead as the plain difference \f$\log\Gamma(\nu/2) - \log\Gamma(\tfrac{\nu+1}{2})\f$, of two log-gammas of size \f$\nu/2\f$, its absolute error would be about \f$\nu\varepsilon/2\f$ (where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is double-precision machine epsilon): measured against mpmath (mpmath), that form carried \f$0.3\f$ ulp of \f$T\f$ at \f$\nu = 3.5\f$, \f$9.3\f$ at \f$\nu = 17\f$ and \f$468\f$ at \f$\nu = 250\f$, and through \f$Z\f$ it set the accuracy of the expected information of both laws, \f$706\f$ ulp against the \f$58.7\f$ they measure now. For `prox_y()`, the tests in `tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_tails.cpp` verify the far-tail shrinkage across all three tails at \f$U \in \{10^2, 10^3, 10^4, 10^5\}\f$ and check the stationarity condition \f$t\,q'(x) + x - U = 0\f$ of `student_tail` out to \f$U = 10^{15}\f$ within \f$10^{-10} U\f$. They also compare all three tails against \f$12243\f$ reference rows from mpmath (mpmath), as reported on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page". Inside a Kokkos kernel the host and the device agree on `prox_y()` within \f$2.28 \times 10^{-16}\f$ relative over \f$24\f$ points for each of four law and tail pairs: the Newton refinements stop on an exact comparison of successive iterates, and the device's fused multiply-add moves the last iterate by no more than that. The degrees of freedom \f$\nu\f$ of `student_tail` are weakly identified when only the tail informs them, so they will often be held fixed; the law admits them as a free parameter.

For the full measurement record, see `docs/numerics.md`.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/properizing_tail.hpp>

void eval_student_tail(Kokkos::View<const double*> x, Kokkos::View<double*> out,
                       double tail_scale, double tail_df) {
  const laplace::distrib::student_tail tail{tail_scale, tail_df};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("student_tail_eval", x.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    const double z = x(i) / tail.varsigma();
    // the exponent H(x) and the logarithm of the mass beyond x
    out(i) = tail.kappa(0) * tail.psi_deriv(0, z) + tail.log_upper(x(i));
  });
}
```

## Tail types

- \ref laplace::distrib::student_tail "student_tail" — Student properizing tail (default), with `tail_scale` and `tail_df`
- \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian_tail "gaussian_tail" — Gaussian properizing tail, with `tail_sigma2`
- \ref laplace::distrib::logistic_tail "logistic_tail" — logistic properizing tail, with `tail_scale`

